        "parameters": {
          "color": 5,
          "content": "# Template configuration\n\n## How this template works\n1. **Set project_key** holds the hardcoded deployment key.\n2. **Get Project Config** fetches the row from `cs_project_config`.\n3. **Restore trigger data** re-emits the trigger payload so Split Out sees `$json.transactionToRefund`.\n4. Downstream HTTP and Slack nodes read `$('Get Project Config').first().json.payments_api_base_url` and `slack_automation_errors_channel_id`.\n\nOther values (zendeskDomain, ticketID, ticketCustomStatusID) are passed from the caller.\n\n## To duplicate for a new project\n- Duplicate this workflow.\n- Change the value in **Set project_key**.\n- Re-bind Zendesk and Slack credentials.",
          "height": 240,
          "width": 700
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "5924e401-8ec8-498b-83c7-9e45fc97bb04",
        "name": "Result",
        "type": "n8n-nodes-base.code",
        "typeVersion": 2,
        "position": [
          1056,
          96
        ],
        "parameters": {
          "jsCode": "// Summary for the caller (PS prod - Refund requests flow), decision 2026-10-15.\n// ok is false when a refund failed. The failure path has already posted an\n// internal note, set the ticket open, set Allow automation to no and alerted\n// Slack; the caller must not send its approval.\nfunction ran(name) {\n  try { return $(name).isExecuted; } catch (e) { return false; }\n}\nconst refundFailed = ran('Reply internally and set status to open');\nreturn [{ json: { ok: !refundFailed, refund_ok: !refundFailed } }];\n"
        },
        "notes": "Last node: tells the caller whether the refund succeeded ({ ok })."
      }
